## Workload Authorization Grant (WAG)

Paul Carleton presented the slides for [Workload Authorization Grant (WAG) - WIMSE Interim](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-wimse-02/materials/slides-interim-2026-wimse-02-sessa-workload-authorization-grant-wag-wimse-interim-00).

### Problem Statement
The motivating problem is that platforms have many workloads (or agents) that need to access third-party systems across a trust boundary. Third-party systems must be able to tell these workloads apart for auditing, applying policy, assigning resource ownership, and managing billing/rate limits. 

WAG aims to standardize the connection from the workload to the third-party system with little-to-no per-workload setup, preventing a default fallback to a single shared API key.

### Technical Presentation
*   **Relationship to OAuth Identity Assertion:** Aaron Parecki explained that WAG builds on the identity assertion JWT authorization grant (RFC 7523) and the OAuth ID-assertion draft. While ID-assertion is designed for human SSO flows, WAG mirrors this pattern for workloads acting on their own behalf without a user present. This allows authorization servers to reuse existing token endpoint logic and trust models.
*   **Workload Identity Federation (WIF) Patterns:** Arndt Schwenkschuster highlighted similarities to industry patterns (e.g., AWS IAM, Google Workload Identity Pools, Azure Managed Identities) using RFC 7523 assertions. WAG leverages centralized trust establishment and standard OpenID Connect discovery to handle access for ephemeral workloads without needing individual life cycle management for every workload in the target system.
*   **Scope:** The draft focuses purely on standardizing the assertion token and its exchange at the token endpoint. Out of scope are: how the workload obtains the WAG, fine-grained authorization, and the specific claims used to convey workload attributes.

